WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.878
Bayesian networks are useful because they support several reasoning directions in one model.

00:00:04.878 --> 00:00:08.186
Predictive reasoning moves from possible causes toward likely effects.

00:00:08.186 --> 00:00:13.332
If a storm becomes more likely, a power interruption may also become more likely.

00:00:13.332 --> 00:00:18.478
If a server is under heavy load, a performance alert may become more likely.

00:00:18.478 --> 00:00:21.786
The model uses parent-to-child relationships to update expected outcomes.

00:00:21.786 --> 00:00:25.462
Diagnostic reasoning moves from an observed effect toward possible causes.

00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:30.241
If a power interruption is observed, the probability of storm damage, equipment fault,

00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:32.814
maintenance activity, or supplier outage may change.

00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:35.019
If a security alert is observed,

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:39.062
the probability of benign misconfiguration and malicious activity may both change,

00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:40.532
depending on the model.

00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:44.943
Diagnostic reasoning is common in troubleshooting, medical screening at a high level,

00:00:44.943 --> 00:00:46.781
fraud review, and incident triage.

00:00:46.781 --> 00:00:49.354
It is not professional advice by itself.

00:00:49.354 --> 00:00:50.824
It is structured uncertainty.

00:00:50.824 --> 00:00:54.500
Intercausal reasoning occurs between alternative causes of a shared effect.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.705
This is where explaining away appears.

00:00:56.705 --> 00:01:01.851
Suppose two different causes can produce the same alarm: Equipment Fault and Sensor Fault.

00:01:01.851 --> 00:01:05.527
Observing the alarm may raise the probability of both causes.

00:01:05.527 --> 00:01:07.732
If later evidence confirms Sensor Fault,

00:01:07.732 --> 00:01:13.246
the probability of Equipment Fault may decrease because the confirmed sensor issue explains the alarm.

00:01:13.246 --> 00:01:16.186
The causes compete to explain the same effect.

00:01:16.186 --> 00:01:19.862
Explaining away is a probability shift, not a courtroom proof.

00:01:19.862 --> 00:01:23.538
The alternative cause may become less likely, but not impossible.

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:26.478
In real models, multiple causes can occur together.

00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:30.154
Probability updates should be communicated with confidence, evidence, and uncertainty.

00:01:30.154 --> 00:01:33.462
Predictive, diagnostic, and intercausal reasoning help teams organize decisions,

00:01:33.462 --> 00:01:37.873
but responsible use requires remembering that updated probabilities are not absolute proof.

00:01:37.873 --> 00:01:40.446
This is especially helpful in operational settings.

00:01:40.446 --> 00:01:45.224
A security alert might be caused by a misconfigured rule, benign user behavior,

00:01:45.224 --> 00:01:47.062
compromised credentials, or noisy telemetry.

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:51.841
A fraud signal might come from ordinary travel, account takeover, data entry error,

00:01:51.841 --> 00:01:53.678
or a changed purchasing pattern.

00:01:53.678 --> 00:01:56.986
The network can update these explanations as evidence arrives,

00:01:56.986 --> 00:01:59.559
but the user still needs judgment, investigation,

00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:02.500
and appropriate escalation before acting on high-impact conclusions.
